("CatBoost",
 "",
 [
 ("Why CatBoost as the baseline?",
  "It is a leading gradient-boosting library, handles categorical features with ordered target statistics, reduces target leakage and uses symmetric trees that resist overfitting. Tuned boosting is the standard any new tabular method must beat.", True),
 ("Explain how gradient boosting works.",
  "Trees are added one at a time. Each new tree is fitted to the negative gradient of the loss (the remaining error) of the current ensemble and added with a learning rate. Many small corrections build a strong model.", True),
 ("What are ordered target statistics and ordered boosting?",
  "When a categorical value is converted to a number using the target, CatBoost only uses records that come earlier in a random order, so a record's own label never leaks into its encoding. Ordered boosting applies the same idea to the gradient steps to reduce prediction shift.", False),
 ("What are oblivious (symmetric) trees?",
  "Trees where every node at the same depth uses the same split. They are fast, and act as a regulariser against overfitting.", False),
 ("How did you tune CatBoost?",
  "Grid search over depth {4, 6, 8} and learning rate {0.01, 0.03, 0.1} (9 combinations), each scored by 5-fold stratified cross-validation on macro-F1, with l2_leaf_reg 3, up to 1,000 iterations and early stopping after 50 rounds. Best: depth 4, learning rate 0.1, CV macro-F1 0.7665.", True),
 ("Your final CatBoost used the test set for early stopping. Isn't that leakage?",
  "Yes, it is a limitation, and I report it in Sections 3.8.4 and 5.2. The test set only chose the number of trees, not the hyperparameters. If anything it could have slightly favoured CatBoost, and CatBoost still did not beat TabPFN, so the main conclusion does not depend on it. The fix is a validation split from the training data.", True),
 ("Why was depth 4 best and not deeper trees?",
  "The relationships are fairly smooth and additive, so shallow trees generalise better; deeper trees overfit. With learning rate 0.1 the model converges in a reasonable number of trees.", False),
 ("Why only a small grid? Why not Bayesian optimisation?",
  "Depth and learning rate are the two parameters that most control complexity and speed; nine combinations kept the search within compute limits. Wider or Bayesian search could add a little accuracy and is a fair extension.", False),
 ]),

("TabPFN and TabFM (foundation models)",
 "Expect conceptual questions. Explain in-context learning simply.",
 [
 ("What is a tabular foundation model?",
  "A transformer pretrained once on millions of synthetic tables so that it learns the general skill of predicting from examples. For a new dataset you give it the labelled rows as context with the query rows, and it predicts in one forward pass, without training or tuning.", True),
 ("What is in-context learning?",
  "Learning from examples placed in the input instead of changing the model's weights. The training rows act like a prompt; the model's attention compares each query row with them.", True),
 ("How is TabPFN different from TabFM?",
  "TabPFN applies attention mainly across samples. TabFM (Google, 2026) alternates attention across rows and across columns, compresses each row, then an in-context transformer predicts. Both are zero-shot; TabFM is newer and much slower at large context in its 1.0.0 release.", True),
 ("What does 'prior-fitted network' mean?",
  "TabPFN is trained on data generated from a prior over many possible datasets (structural causal models). It learns to approximate the Bayesian posterior predictive distribution, so given the training set it outputs class probabilities directly.", False),
 ("Why did TabFM use only 500 rows?",
  "Its prediction time grew steeply with context size: about 0.11 s per record at 500 rows and 1.8 s at 2,000. The full 17,284-row run did not finish within cloud session limits. 500 rows was the largest size that completed on the full test set. I then compared all models at matched sizes to be fair.", True),
 ("Isn't comparing TabFM with 500 rows against CatBoost with 17,284 rows unfair?",
  "It is unequal, which is why I added the matched-context experiment (100, 500 and 2,000 rows for all models). At every matched size both foundation models beat CatBoost. The full-size comparison favours CatBoost, yet TabFM was still not significantly worse.", True),
 ("TabPFN was designed for small data (up to 10,000 samples). You used 17,284.",
  "Yes, beyond its reported range. It still matched CatBoost, and it did not exceed it, which suggests its advantage is largest with small data and disappears as data grows. That is consistent with my scaling results.", True),
 ("Why do foundation models need so little data?",
  "They bring prior knowledge from pretraining on many datasets, so they need fewer examples to form a good decision rule. Trees must learn splits from scratch and need enough records in each region. I did not test this explanation directly.", True),
 ("What are the drawbacks of foundation models?",
  "Prediction cost grows with context size, the training data must be available at prediction time, they are harder to explain (need model-agnostic SHAP), and new packages can have defects. TabPFN in my app also needs a cloud API token.", True),
 ("What is attention? Write the formula.",
  "Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax(Q K-transpose / sqrt(d_k)) V. Each element's query is compared with all keys; the softmax weights decide how much of each value is used.", False),
 ]),

("Evaluation metrics and statistics",
 "Very common. Be precise with definitions.",
 [
 ("Why macro-averaged metrics instead of accuracy alone?",
  "The classes are unequal (45/35/20). Macro-averaging computes each metric per class and averages with equal weight, so the smaller High tier counts as much as Low.", True),
 ("Define precision, recall and F1.",
  "Precision: of records predicted as a class, the share that truly are. Recall: of records truly in a class, the share found. F1: harmonic mean of precision and recall, high only when both are high.", True),
 ("What does ROC-AUC of 0.92 mean for a three-class problem?",
  "I used one-vs-rest: for each tier, the probability that a random record of that tier gets a higher score for it than a random record of the other tiers, averaged over the three tiers. 0.92 means very good ranking.", True),
 ("What is McNemar's test and why did you use it?",
  "A test for two classifiers on the same test records. It uses only disagreements: b records the first got right and the second wrong, c the reverse. Chi-square = (|b - c| - 1)^2 / (b + c). It suits models trained once on one split and has a low false-positive rate (Dietterich, 1998).", True),
 ("Your p-values were not significant. Does that mean the models are equal?",
  "No. It means no difference was detected; it is not proof of equivalence. To claim equivalence I would need an equivalence test (for example TOST) with a chosen margin.", True),
 ("What are Wilson confidence intervals?",
  "An interval for a proportion that behaves well even near 0 or 1 and for small samples. Mine are about +/-1.2 points on 4,322 records, and the three intervals overlap almost completely.", False),
 ("Why did you also report kappa and quadratic weighted kappa?",
  "The tiers are ordered. Kappa corrects accuracy for chance (0.65-0.66). Quadratic weighted kappa penalises big errors more (Low vs High counts 4x a neighbouring error); 0.80-0.81 shows almost all errors are between neighbours.", False),
 ("Why not statistical tests at reduced context sizes?",
  "I did not run them, so I make no significance claims there. The 2,000-row run used only 500 test records, where the interval is about +/-3.7 points. It is listed as future work.", True),
 ("What is 33.3% and 45% that you mention?",
  "33.3% is random guessing among three classes; 45% is always predicting the largest class (Low). All models reached about 78%, far above both baselines.", False),
 ]),

