("Explainability (SHAP)",
 "",
 [
 ("What is SHAP?",
  "Shapley Additive Explanations: from game theory, each feature is a player and the prediction is the payout. The prediction equals the baseline plus the sum of each feature's contribution. It is the only additive method satisfying local accuracy, missingness and consistency.", True),
 ("Which features were most important?",
  "The same seven in all three models: iron, stress level, total protein, vitamin D, ALT (liver), calcium and manganese. High stress and ALT raise High risk; high iron, protein, vitamin D, calcium and manganese lower it.", True),
 ("Why TreeExplainer for CatBoost but KernelExplainer for the others?",
  "TreeExplainer computes exact values using the tree structure and is fast. TabPFN and TabFM have no trees, so a model-agnostic method was needed. The planned PermutationExplainer failed with a numba error on both Colab and Kaggle, so I used KernelExplainer.", True),
 ("Only 20-30 records for the foundation-model SHAP. Is that enough?",
  "It is enough to see which features lead and their direction, which agree with CatBoost's full-data SHAP and with the data correlations. It is not enough to trust the exact order, so I do not interpret rank differences. Larger samples are future work.", True),
 ("Can SHAP show causes?",
  "No. SHAP explains what the model uses, not causal effects. A model that learned a spurious pattern will give an explanation of that pattern.", True),
 ("What do beeswarm and waterfall plots show?",
  "Beeswarm: every record's contribution per feature, coloured by feature value, showing direction. Waterfall: for one patient, how each feature pushes the prediction from the baseline to the final value.", False),
 ("How would a doctor use your explanations?",
  "For one patient the waterfall plot lists which factors raised the risk (for example high stress, low iron) and which lowered it, so the doctor can check it against clinical judgement and give targeted advice.", False),
 ("Why did you compare SHAP with correlations?",
  "As an independent check that does not depend on the models: the seven top SHAP features are exactly the seven most correlated with risk, and the two features with zero correlation have zero importance.", False),
 ]),

